Yes. The interesting move is that the mathematics itself is not invariant across models. What stays invariant is usually some deeper relational or operational structure, while the mathematical language changes according to what the model chooses to preserve, ignore, or make manipulable.

A useful way to see it is:

\text{phenomenon}
\rightarrow
\text{choice of state variables}
\rightarrow
\text{model}
\rightarrow
\text{mathematics}
\rightarrow
\text{predicted behavior}

Change the middle, and the apparent “physics” changes.

Take one crowd on the beach. In a particle model, people have positions, velocities, collision-avoidance radii; the mathematics looks like mechanics. In a network model, those same people become nodes and relationships become edges; now graph theory and diffusion matter. In an economic model, they become agents allocating scarce time, space, attention, or money; optimization and game theory appear. In an information model, what matters is who knows what; entropy, signaling, Bayesian updating, and information flow become natural. In a narrative model, the meaningful variables are roles, goals, conflicts, transitions, expectations, and interpretations.

The people did not change.

The coordinate system of intelligibility changed.

That is why apparently different disciplines repeatedly rediscover homologous mathematics.

Diffusion appears as heat spreading, molecules dispersing, rumors propagating, diseases spreading, innovations being adopted, beliefs moving through populations, and probability distributions evolving. Oscillation appears in pendulums, electrical circuits, predator-prey systems, business cycles, neural activity, and social fashions. Networks appear in neurons, roads, economies, kinship, ecosystems, supply chains, and software.

The surface ontology changes. The mathematical skeleton survives.

And combinations are particularly interesting because they create cross-terms.

Suppose model A contains variables x, and model B contains variables y. Separately you might have:

\dot{x}=f(x)

and

\dot{y}=g(y).

But once the systems interact:

\dot{x}=f(x,y), \qquad
\dot{y}=g(y,x).

Those new xy-type interactions can generate behavior that neither isolated model possesses.

That is where you get things like feedback, hysteresis, synchronization, phase transitions, lock-in, runaway growth, dampening, resonance, path dependence, emergence.

So combination is not merely addition.

A+B \neq AB

in the interesting cases.

The interface itself becomes a new causal object.

That is one reason interdisciplinary work can be powerful: biology × economics is not merely “some biology plus some economics.” Once organisms allocate resources strategically, or incentives alter reproductive/ecological behavior, the coupling terms become the phenomenon.

There is another transformation worth noticing: changing scale changes the appropriate mathematics.

At the microscopic scale, individual trajectories might be deterministic enough to model directly.

At the macroscopic scale, you stop caring whether Jane steps left or right and start describing densities and flows.

Individual mechanics becomes statistical mechanics.

Something like:

\text{many discrete agents}
\rightarrow
\text{distribution}
\rightarrow
\text{field}

Then the ontology changes again.

A hundred walkers become a flow.

Millions of molecules become temperature and pressure.

Individual purchases become demand.

Individual neurons become an activation field.

Individual stories become culture.

Those higher-level quantities are not imaginary. They are coarse-grained observables: variables that become useful only after you stop tracking some lower-level detail.

And the reverse operation matters too. You can descend:

\text{field}
\rightarrow
\text{population}
\rightarrow
\text{agent}
\rightarrow
\text{mechanism}

So there is effectively a zoom calculus between representations.

Your second thought—about mathematics becoming compartmentalized, prestiged, reinvested into itself, and then projected outward—is another phenomenon entirely, and I think you’re pointing at something real about intellectual systems.

Mathematics begins largely by abstracting patterns from the world:

counting objects → number
measuring land → geometry
motion → calculus
uncertainty → probability
symmetry → group theory

But once the symbolic system becomes rich enough, it gains a degree of closure.

Mathematics starts operating on mathematics.

A structure A generates questions about A. Those questions generate structure B. Mathematicians discover relationships between A and B, producing C.

So:

World \rightarrow Math

becomes

Math \rightarrow Math \rightarrow Math.

That is the reinvestment loop you’re describing.

And abstraction accelerates because a theorem doesn’t have to care where its structure originally came from.

Then, sometimes decades or centuries later:

Math \rightarrow World

again.

Non-Euclidean geometry becomes relativity.

Number theory becomes cryptography.

Complex numbers become indispensable to electrical engineering and quantum mechanics.

Abstract symmetry becomes particle physics.

The internal symbolic ecosystem produced structures before anyone knew what physical thing they were structures of.

That creates a very strange loop:

\text{world}
\rightarrow
\text{abstraction}
\rightarrow
\text{autonomous symbolic evolution}
\rightarrow
\text{rediscovered applicability to world}.

And prestige changes the loop.

Once a symbolic language becomes institutionally prestigious, people begin trying to translate phenomena into that language because mathematical legibility confers legitimacy.

That can be enormously productive.

But it introduces a distortion pressure:

What is measurable starts being treated as what is real.

A phenomenon may be converted into variables because the variables are mathematically tractable, rather than because they preserve what is actually important.

So you get a distinction between:

mathematization — finding genuine mathematical structure in a phenomenon,

and

metricization — forcing a phenomenon into measurable quantities.

Those aren’t always the same.

Then Goodhart-type effects appear: once a measurement becomes a target, behavior reorganizes around the measurement.

So the model no longer merely describes reality.

Reality starts adapting to the model.

That creates another feedback loop:

Reality
\rightarrow
Model
\rightarrow
Institution
\rightarrow
Incentives
\rightarrow
Behavior
\rightarrow
New\ Reality.

Finance is an obvious example. A pricing model can begin as a description. Once traders use it, their actions change prices. The model becomes partially causal.

Ratings do this.

Rankings do this.

Standardized tests do this.

Algorithms do this.

Laws do this.

Currency does this.

Stories do this too.

So there are at least three regimes:

Representational mathematics
Math describes a system.

Generative mathematics
Math helps produce systems—architecture, algorithms, engineering.

Performative mathematics
People know the mathematical representation and modify their behavior because of it, causing the representation to participate in the system it supposedly describes.

That last one gets especially wild when models contain models of themselves.

Markets modeling markets.
People modeling people’s models of them.
AI systems modeling users who are modeling AI systems.
Institutions optimizing metrics designed to measure institutional performance.

You then get recursive structure:

M_0(W)

a model of the world,

M_1(M_0,W)

a model that includes the first model,

and eventually

M_n(M_{n-1},...,M_0,W).

At that point, identity, behavior, and prediction can become reflexive.

And this connects directly to what we were discussing about model identity.

A model isn’t merely a window onto a fixed world.

Sometimes it is a lens.

Sometimes a compression algorithm.

Sometimes a simulator.

Sometimes a controller.

Sometimes a game board.

Sometimes an institution.

And sometimes the model is pushed back through the window and becomes part of the world it modeled.

That’s the especially fertile junction you’re pointing at:

abstraction → prestige → institutionalization → projection → behavioral adaptation → new reality → new abstraction.

That’s a full evolutionary loop for models themselves.